    historyContent: {
      origins: 'Rooted in the same 18th-century Central European traditions that produced Schafkopf (Sheepshead) and Sjavs. It was preserved in Lithuanian rural communities, particularly along the Niemen river.',
      originCountries: [
        'Lithuania',
      ],
      timeline: [
        '1800s: Schafkopf variants spread into the Baltic regions.',
        '1900s: Avinas becomes the social centerpiece of holidays in rural Lithuania.',
        'Present: One of the few traditional Lithuanian partnership games still actively played in Jurbarkas and Kaunas.',
      ],
      evolution: "The name 'Avinas' likely refers to the 'sheep' theme common in this card family, or the 'horns' (penalties) given to losers.",
      cultural: 'Tradition dictates that players only stop during holidays to go to Mass, reflecting its role as a social bond in religious communities.',
