          dealing: 'Cards are dealt face down in two rounds of 4. If a 7 is accidentally dealt face up, it determines the trump suit and the declarer.',
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: "Capture at least 61 points in tricks to avoid being 'Horned' (penalized).",
          gameplay: "1. **Determining Trump**: If no 7 was flipped during dealing, the dealer determines trump or it follows a rotation. 2. **Tricks**: Players must follow suit. If they cannot, they may trump or discard. 3. **The 'Ram'**: The goal is to reach 61 points. If the declarer's side fails, they receive one 'Horn'. If they fail to get 31 (Schneider), they receive two 'Horns'. 4. **Card Points**: Ace (11), 10 (10), King (4), Queen (3), Jack (2). The Sevens used as trumps count 0 points.",
          keyRules: [
            'Total points in the deck is 120; 60-60 results in a win for the non-bidding side.',
            'The Seven as a high trump is a unique feature that changes the standard Schafkopf strategy.',
            'If a player is dealt two 7s face up during a botched deal, the first one seen usually determines trumps.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'Protect your Queen of Clubs! It is the only invincible card in the game. Use it to capture a Ten or Ace early.',
          intermediate: "The Power of the Seven: Because the 7 of trumps is the second highest card, it can 'trap' every other Queen (except Clubs) and all the Jacks. Do not waste the 7 on a small trick.",
          advanced: "Defensive Lead: If your partnership is in the lead and you hold the QC, lead a small card of an off-suit to see if your partner can trump in. If the opponent trumps, you can 'over-trump' with the QC.",
          tips: [
            "Keep track of the 120 total. If you have 30 points and your partner has 31, you've reached safety.",
            'In Avinas, tens are worth 10 points—as much as a King and Queen combined. Capturing tens is the secondary priority after winning tricks.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
